{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>On that day YHWH cut a covenant with Abram, saying</b> — The Holy One, blessed be He, promised Abraham the gift of the land many times, and all of them for a necessary purpose. When he first came into the land, He said to him, \"To your seed I will give this land\" (Genesis 12:7), and He did not specify His gift, for the verse implies only the area through which he traveled in the land as far as the place of Shechem, as far as Elon Moreh. Afterward, when his merits in the land had increased, He added: \"Lift up now your eyes and look northward and southward and eastward and westward\" (Genesis 13:14), for He would give him all those lands in their entirety. And the meaning is not [limited to] what you see with your eyes, for a person's sight does not extend to a great distance; rather, [the meaning is] that He would give him, for every direction his eyes beheld, [the corresponding land], or that He showed him all the land of Israel as He did with Moses our master. And in this second blessing He further added \"to your seed forever\" and that He would multiply his seed like the dust of the earth. And the third time He specified for him the boundaries of the land, and He mentioned to him all the nations, ten nations, and added to cut a covenant with him concerning them, so that sin would not cause [the gift to be voided]. And when He commanded him concerning circumcision, He said to him, \"for an everlasting possession\" (Genesis 17:8), meaning that even if they are exiled from it, they will return and inherit it. And He added, \"and I will be their Elohim,\" which means that He in His glory will govern them and they will not be under the dominion of star or constellation or any prince of the celestial beings, as will be explained further in the Torah (Leviticus 18:25). And Scripture states in the first instance, \"To your seed I will give\" — in the future tense; and likewise in the second instance, for until then the land had not been given to him in its entirety, and therefore He said to him \"I will give it.\" But in the third instance, at the time of the covenant, He said, \"To your seed I have given,\" meaning that He would cut a covenant with him concerning the gift that He had already given him. And likewise at the time of circumcision, when He said \"for an everlasting possession,\" He said to him \"and I will give to you\" — in the future tense. And Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 15:18): \"'To your seed I have given' — the expression of the Most High is as though it were already done.\" And there is no need for this here:"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:21:10.503727660Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}